  "answer_text": "Here is an overview of the major inventions and contributions of **Hipparchus** (c. 190\u2013120 BC), the ancient Greek astronomer and mathematician:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd2d Who Was Hipparchus?\nHe is often called the **\"father of astronomy\"**, a title conferred on him by Jean Baptiste Joseph Delambre in 1817. He was a Greek astronomer and mathematician who made fundamental contributions to the advancement of astronomy as a mathematical science and to the foundations of trigonometry.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcd0 1. Trigonometry\nHe made an early contribution to trigonometry by producing a **table of chords**, an early example of a trigonometric table; indeed, some historians go so far as to say that trigonometry was **invented by him**. The purpose of this table of chords was to give a method for solving triangles which avoided solving each triangle from first principles.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf0d 2. Division of a Circle into 360 Degrees\nHipparchus also **introduced the division of a circle into 360 degrees** into Greece.\n\n---\n\n### \u2b50 3. First Star Catalog\nHe is credited with creating the **first reliable star catalog**, which included approximately 850 stars and established a system of **stellar magnitude**. Using a thin tube called a *dioptra* to scan the heavens, Hipparchus made extensive observations of star positions and produced the first-known catalog of stars.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf10 4. Precession of the Equinoxes\nHipparchus **discovered the precession of the equinoxes** by comparing his notes with earlier observers; his realization that the points of solstice and equinox moved slowly from east to west against the fixed background of the stars was one of his most significant achievements. He also calculated the **length of the year to within 6.5 minutes**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf19 5. Models of the Sun and Moon\nHe was the **first Greek to develop quantitative and accurate models for the motion of the Sun and Moon**, making use of the observations and knowledge accumulated over centuries by the Chaldeans from Babylonia. Based on his solar and lunar theories and his numerical trigonometry, he was probably the **first to develop a reliable method to predict solar eclipses**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf10 6. Latitude and Longitude\nHe **developed tools for measuring celestial angles** and contributed to the systematic use of geographic coordinates, **pioneering the application of latitude and longitude**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd27 7. The Astrolabe and Astronomical Instruments\nHipparchus is credited with the **invention or improvement of several astronomical instruments**, which were used for a long time for naked-eye observations. During his career, he may have invented the **planispheric astrolabe**, a device on which the celestial sphere is projected onto the plane of the equator.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcbb 8. Possible Inventor of the Antikythera Mechanism\nHe is one of the strongest contenders for the honor of being the **inventor of the Antikythera Mechanism** (also known as the Antikythera Device), considered the world's first analogue computer.\n\n---\n\nIn summary, Hipparchus was a towering figure in ancient science, with groundbreaking contributions spanning astronomy, mathematics, geography, and instrument-making \u2014 many of which laid the foundation for future scientists like Ptolemy and beyond.",
